          %p
            Notably
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mapping+spaces"} mapping spaces
            play a pivotal role in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spaces+of+field+histories"} spaces of field histories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometry
            based on just
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifolds"} manifolds
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} Topos theory
            provides their existence (Prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PropertiesOfSheafToposes"}
            below) and the relevant infrastructure, for example for the construction of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transgression+of+differential+forms"} transgression of differential forms
            to mapping spaces of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+sets"} smooth sets
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+theories"} field theories
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics+--+smooth+sets"} on smooth sets
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics+--+supergeometry"} on supergeometry
          %p
            In conclusion, one motivation for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
            is
            = succeed ":" do
              %em a posteriori
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics"} geometry of physics
          %p
            But we may offer also an
            %em a priori
            motivation:
          %p#CategoryTheoryIsTheoryOfDuality
            %em Category theory is the theory of duality.
          .float_right_image{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 10px 20px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/DualityGraphics.jpg", :width => "200px"}/
          %p
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality"} Duality
            is of course an ancient notion in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/philosophy"} philosophy
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} theory
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+physics"} fundamental physics
            %a{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory#Duff96"} formerly known
            as
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality+in+string+theory"} duality in string theory
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "adjoint+functor#Lambek82"} Lambek 82
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality"} duality
            is the mathematical concept of
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functor"} adjunction
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+triples"} adjoint triples
            (Remark
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#AdjointTriples"}
            below) and their induced
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+modalities"} adjoint modalities
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Some+Thoughts+on+the+Future+of+Category+Theory"}> Lawvere 91
            , see Def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#AdjointModality"}
            below), which exhibit a given “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modality"}> mode of being
            ” of any object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            as intermediate between two dual opposite extremes (Prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ComparisonMorphismBetweenOppositeExtremes"}
            below):
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo □
                  %mi X
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mphantom
                      %mi AAAA
                  %mi X
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mphantom
                      %mi AAAA
                  %mo ◯
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Box X
                  \\overset{\phantom{AAAA}}{\longrightarrow}
                  X
                  \\overset{\phantom{AAAA}}{\longrightarrow}
                  \\bigcirc X
          %p
            For example,
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesion"} cohesive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometric
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+spaces"} generalized spaces
            is captured, this way, as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modality"} modality
            in between the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+object"} discrete
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codiscrete+object"} codiscrete
            (Example
            = succeed "," do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DiscreteTopologicalSpaces"}
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CohesiveTopos"}
            below).
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 40px 10px 0px; float: left"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/CategoryNotionsHierarchy.png", :width => "280px"}/
          %p#HierarchyOfNotion
            Historically,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            was introduced in order to make precise the concept of
            = succeed ":" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural transformation
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
            was introduced just so as to support that of natural transformations, and the concept of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            only served that of functors (see
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "category+theory#Freyd64"} Freyd 1964,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/reprints/articles/3/tr3.pdf#page=27"} p 1
          %p
            But natural transformations are, in turn, exactly the basis for the concept of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functors"} adjoint functors
            (Def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#AdjointFunctorsInTermsOfNaturalBijectionOfHomSets"}
            below), equivalently
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunctions"} adjunctions
              between categories
            (Prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#AdjointnessInTermsOfHomIsomorphismEquivalentToAdjunctionInCat"}
            below).
          %p
            Shown below is the “Yin-Yang identity” (the
            = succeed "," do
              %em triangle identity
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "##AdjointnessInTermsOfHomIsomorphismEquivalentToAdjunctionInCat"}
            below) characterizing
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunctions"} adjunctions
          .float_right_image{:style => "margin: -40px 0px 10px 20px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/Adjointness.jpg", :width => "460px"}/
          %p
            All
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+constructions"} universal constructions
            — the heart of category theory — are special cases of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functors"} adjoint functors
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "adjoint+functor#Lambek82"} Lambek 82
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits"} limits
            and
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimits"} colimits
            (Def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#Limits"}
            below),
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ends"} ends
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coends"} coends
            (Def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#EndAndCoendInTopcgSmash"}
            below)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+extensions"} Kan extensions
            (Prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TopologicalLeftKanExtensionBCoend"}
            below), and the behaviour of these constructions, such as for instance the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+co-completion"} free co-completion
            nature of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+embedding"} Yoneda embedding
            (Prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FreeCocompletion"}
            below).
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            Therefore it makes sense to regard category theory as the
            = succeed "," do
              %strong theory of adjunctions
            %br/
            hence the
            = succeed ":" do
