            .proof
              %h6#proof_5 Proof
              %p
                We discuss the case of the path space object. The other case is
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+dual"} formally dual
              %p
                First, that the component maps are weak equivalences follows generally: by definition they have a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+inverse"} right inverse
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Path
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Path(X) \to X
                and so this follows by
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/two-out-of-three"} two-out-of-three
                (def.
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CategoryWithWeakEquivalences"}
              %p
                But if
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                is fibrant, then also the two projection maps out of the product
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ×
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \times X \to X
                are fibrations, because they are both pullbacks of the fibration
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to \ast
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mtable
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mi X
                              %mo ×
                              %mi X
                            %mtd
                              %mo ⟶
