            %p
              (
              %a{:href => "#Brown73"}> Brown 73, section 4, theorem 3
              )
            .proof
              %h6#proof_36 Proof
              %p
                Given an object
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ∈
                      %msup
                        %mi 𝒞
                        %mrow
                          %mo *
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \in \mathcal{C}^{\ast/}
                and given two choices of path space objects
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Path
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Path(X)
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mover
                          %mrow
                            %mi Path
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi X
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ˜
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widetilde{Path(X)}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Ho
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi 𝒞
                        %mrow
                          %mo *
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ho(\mathcal{C}^{\ast/})
                between
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega X
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mover
                          %mi Ω
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde \Omega X
              %p
                To that end, first lemma
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ReplacementOfPathObjects"}
                implies that any two choices of path space objects are connected via a third path space by a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/span"} span
                of morphisms compatible with the structure maps. By
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/two-out-of-three"} two-out-of-three
                (def.
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CategoryWithWeakEquivalences"}
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#BaseChangePreservesFibrationsAndWeakEquivalences"}
                implies that these morphisms induce weak equivalences on the corresponding loop space objects. This shows that all choices of loop space objects become isomorphic in the homotopy category.
              %p
                Moreover, all the isomorphisms produced this way are actually equal: this follows from lemma
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#UniquenessOfFibersOfQualizedMorphismsInHoC"}
                applied to
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mtable
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mi X
                            %mtd
                              %mover
                                %mo ⟶
                                %mi s
                            %mtd
                              %mi Path
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi X
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mtd
                              %mover
                                %mo ⟶
                                %mo ⟶
                            %mtd
                              %mover
                                %mrow
                                  %mi Path
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                  %mi X
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                %mo ˜
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %mi X
                              %mo ×
                              %mi X
                            %mtd
                              %mover
                                %mo ⟶
                                %mi id
                            %mtd
                              %mi X
                              %mo ×
                              %mi X
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\array{
                      X &amp;\overset{s}{\longrightarrow}&amp; Path(X) &amp;\stackrel{\longrightarrow}{\longrightarrow}&amp; \widetilde{Path(X)}
                      \\\
                      \&amp;&amp; \downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                      \\\
                      \&amp;&amp; X\times X &amp;\overset{id}{\longrightarrow}&amp;  X \times X
                      }
                      \\,.
              %p This way we obtain a functor
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Ω
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %msubsup
                        %mi 𝒞
                        %mi f
                        %mrow
                          %mo *
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mi Ho
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi 𝒞
                        %mrow
                          %mo *
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\Omega \;\colon\; \mathcal{C}^{\ast/}_f \longrightarrow Ho(\mathcal{C}^{\ast/})
                      \\,.
              %p
                By prop.
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FiberOfFibrationIsCompatibleWithWeakEquivalences"}
                (and using that Cartesian product preserves weak equivalences) this functor sends weak equivalences to isomorphisms. Therefore the functor on homotopy categories now follows with theorem
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#UniversalPropertyOfHomotopyCategoryOfAModelCategory"}
              %p
                It is immediate to see that the operation of loop concatenation from remark
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ConcatenatedLoopSpaceObject"}
                gives the objects
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi X
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi Ho
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi 𝒞
                        %mrow
                          %mo *
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega X \in Ho(\mathcal{C}^{\ast/})
                the structure of
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoids"} monoids
              %p We claim that the inverse-assigning operation is given by the left map in the following pasting composite
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mtable
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mi Ω
                              %mo ′
                              %mi X
                            %mtd
                              %mo ⟶
                            %mtd
                              %mi Path
                              %mo ′
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi X
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mtd
                              %mover
                                %mo ⟶
                                %mrow
                            %mtd
                              %mi X
                              %mo ×
                              %mi X
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %msup
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                                %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                  %mo ≃
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %msup
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                                %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                  %mo ≃
                            %mtd
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi pb
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mtd
                              %msup
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                                %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                  %mi swap
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mi Ω
                              %mi X
                            %mtd
                              %mo ⟶
                            %mtd
                              %mi Path
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi X
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mtd
                              %munder
                                %mo ⟶
                                %mrow
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                  %msub
                                    %mi p
                                    %mn 0
                                  %mo ,
                                  %msub
                                    %mi p
                                    %mn 1
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mtd
                              %mi X
                              %mo ×
                              %mi X
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ,
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\array{
                      \\Omega' X &amp;\longrightarrow&amp; Path'(X) &amp;\overset{}{\longrightarrow}&amp; X \times X
                      \\\
                      \\downarrow^{\mathrlap{\simeq}} &amp;&amp; \downarrow^{\mathrlap{\simeq}} &amp;(pb)&amp; \downarrow^{\mathrlap{swap}}
                      \\\
                      \\Omega X &amp;\longrightarrow&amp; Path(X) &amp;\underset{(p_0,p_1)}{\longrightarrow}&amp; X \times X
                      }
                      \\,,
              %p
                (where
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Path
                        %mo ′
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Path'(X)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Path
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Path(X)
                by “reversing the notion of source and target of a path”).
              %p To see that this is indeed an inverse, it is sufficient to see that the two morphisms
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi X
                      %mover
                        %mo ⟶
                        %mo ⟶
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi X
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\Omega X \stackrel{\longrightarrow}{\longrightarrow} (\Omega X)_2
              %p induced from
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mtable
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mi Path
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi X
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mover
                                %munder
                                  %mo ⟶
                                  %mrow
                                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                    %mi s
                                    %mo ∘
                                    %msub
                                      %mi p
                                      %mn 0
                                    %mo ,
                                    %mi s
                                    %mo ∘
                                    %msub
                                      %mi p
                                      %mn 0
                                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                %mover
                                  %mo ⟶
                                  %mi Δ
                              %mi Path
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi X
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %msub
                                %mo ×
                                %mi X
                              %mi Path
                              %mo ′
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi X
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\array{
                      Path(X)
                      \\stackrel{\overset{\Delta}{\longrightarrow}}{\underset{(s\circ p_0,s \circ p_0)}{\longrightarrow}}
                      Path(X) \times_X Path'(X)
                      }
              %p
                coincide in the homotopy category. This follows with lemma
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#UniquenessOfFibersOfQualizedMorphismsInHoC"}
                applied to the following commuting diagram:
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mtable
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mi X
                            %mtd
                              %mover
                                %mo ⟶
                                %mi i
                            %mtd
                              %mi Path
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi X
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mtd
                              %mover
                                %munder
                                  %mo ⟶
                                  %mrow
                                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                    %mi s
                                    %mo ∘
                                    %msub
                                      %mi p
                                      %mn 0
                                    %mo ,
                                    %mi s
                                    %mo ∘
                                    %msub
                                      %mi p
                                      %mn 0
                                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                %mover
                                  %mo ⟶
                                  %mi Δ
