              %p
                Hence the pointed analog of prop.
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PushoutProductInTopCGSendsCofCofToCof"}
                holds and therefore so does the pointed analog of the conclusion in prop.
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#HomProductAdjunctionForCofibrantObjectInTopCGIsQuillen"}
            %h3#ModelStructureOnTopEnrichedFunctors Model structure on topological functors
            %p
              With classical topological homotopy theory in hand (theorem
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TopQuillenModelStructure"}
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ClassicalModelStructureOnCompactlyGeneratedTopologicalSpaces"}
              = succeed "." do
                %em topological diagrams
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+theory"} stable homotopy theory
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectra"} spectra
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "Introduction+to+Stable+homotopy+theory+--+1-1#SequentialSpectraAsDiagramSpectra"} prop.
            %p
              Technically, “topological diagram” here means “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"}> Top
              \-
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+functor"}> enriched functor
              ”. We now discuss what this means and then observe that as an immediate corollary of theorem
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TopQuillenModelStructure"}
              we obtain a model category structure on topological diagrams.
            %p
              As a by-product, we obtain the model category theory of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+colimits"} homotopy colimits
              in topological spaces, which will be useful.
            %p
              In the following we say
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"}> Top
                \-
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} enriched category
              and
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"}> Top
                \-
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+functor"} enriched functor
              etc. for what often is referred to as “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+category"}> topological category
              ” and “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+functor"}> topological functor
              ” etc. As discussed there, these latter terms are ambiguous.
            %p
              %strong Literature
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Categorical+Homotopy+Theory"}> Riehl, chapter 3
              ) for basics of
              = succeed ";" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category+theory"} enriched category theory
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "#Piacenza91"} Piacenza 91
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+model+structure+on+functors"} projective model structure on topological functors
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
            #TopEnrichedCategory.num_defn
