                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom(S^q \times I,-)
                (and similarly for the pointed case). This implies the claimed isomorphism on homotopy groups.
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
            %h2#SimplicialHomotopyTheory
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
              \-Groupoids II): Simplicial homotopy theory
            %p
              With
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoids"} groupoids
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complexes"} chain complexes
              we have seen two kinds of objects which support concepts of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+equivalence"} homotopy equivalence
              between them (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+groupoids"} equivalence of groupoids
              on the one hand, and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-isomorphism"} quasi-isomorphism
              on the other). In some sense these two cases are opposite extremes in the more general context of
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complexes"} chain complexes
                  have homotopical structure (e.g.
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+homology"} chain homology
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+n-types"} homotopy n-types
                  for arbitrary
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                  %em abelian
                  in that there is never any
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonabelian+group"} nonabelian group
                  structure in a chain complex, not is there any non-trivial
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
                  of the homology groups of a chain complex on each other;
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoids"} groupoids
                  have more general non-abelian structure, for every (
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonabelian+group"}> nonabelian
                  )
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
                  there is a groupoid which has this as its
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+group"} fundamental group
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+1-types"} homotopy 1-types
            %p
              On the other hand, both groupoids and chain complexes naturally have incarnations in the joint context of
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+sets"} simplicial sets
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complexes"} Kan complexes
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
